kets":"India - metros, emerging cities and Tier II/III markets|Thailand (international business)|Nepal (international business)|Nigeria (international business)|Dine-in, takeaway and delivery channels|Food courts, airports and other travel/high-footfall locations","expansion":"DIL's expansion in FY 2025-26 was network- and platform-led rather than plant-led. As of March 31, 2026 the Company operated 2,256 stores across India, Nepal, Nigeria and Thailand, up from 2,039 a year earlier, spanning dine-in, takeaway, delivery, travel hubs and other convenience-led formats and supported by capabilities in site selection, supply chain management and procurement. Inorganically, the Company acquired Sky Gate Hospitality Private Limited during the year - discharging part of the consideration for a ~80.72% fully-diluted equity stake through a preferential allotment of equity …","grades":{"rel":0,"s":"","wm90":null},"industry":"Leisure Services","isin":"INE872J01023","kpi":null,"listed_on":"2021-08-16","litigation":{"_nulled":"P0 2026-08-08 — unevidenced verdict removed (prompt-seeded default)","material":null,"note":"Not determinable from the annual-report bundle: the contingent-liability note, the auditor's report and the CARO annexure are not routed to this reader, so no litigation evidence was available. Absence here is OUR coverage gap, not a statement that the company has no litigation."},"mdna":"Consumer demand in food services remained relatively subdued through much of the first three quarters as discretionary spending moderated, requiring sharper execution and greater operational discipline|The fourth quarter witnessed early signs of recovery, with improving consumption trends across several markets supported by government measures to stimulate demand and improving consumer confidence|Value-led initiatives at KFC together with continued product innovation across brands resonated with customers and contributed to improving average daily sales and a gradual stabilisation in …","objects":null,"oem":"","one_liner":"Devyani International Limited (DIL) is one of India's largest quick-service restaurant operators and the largest franchisee of Yum! Brands in India and Nepal, building a multi-brand, omni-channel food and beverage platform under the RJ Corp Group.","peer_panel":{"asof":"2026-08-14","industry":"Leisure Services","peers":[{"ebitda_margin":19.9,"fy":"FY26","growth_basis":"7y CAGR","mcap":32464,"name":"JUBILANT FOODWORKS LTD","pat":444.2,"pat_growth":4.8,"pe":73.1,"rev":9512.5,"rev_growth":15.1,"s":"JUBLFOOD","source":"product","why":"shares chicken/food/pizza"},{"ebitda_margin":12.8,"fy":"FY26","growth_basis":"7y CAGR","mcap":7656,"name":"WESTLIFE FOODWORLD LTD","pat":32.3,"pat_growth":6.1,"pe":237.0,"rev":2625.6,"rev_growth":9.4,"s":"WESTLIFE","source":"product","why":"shares coffee/food/pizza"},{"ebitda_margin":15.2,"fy":"FY26","growth_basis":"YoY","mcap":6215,"name":"SAPPHIRE FOODS INDIA LTD","pat":-31.9,"pat_growth":-265.9,"pe":null,"rev":3125.3,"rev_growth":25.1,"s":"SAPPHIRE","source":"product","why":"shares brand/chicken/food/indian"},{"ebitda_margin":14.4,"fy":"FY26","growth_basis":"","mcap":928,"name":"UNITED FOODBRANDS LIMITED","pat":-61.9,"pat_growth":null,"pe":null,"rev":1338.7,"rev_growth":7.9,"s":"UFBL","source":"product","why":"shares brand/food/multi/service"},{"ebitda_margin":17.4,"fy":"FY26","growth_basis":"YoY","mcap":488,"name":"SPECIALITY REST LTD","pat":20.7,"pat_growth":-4.6,"pe":23.6,"rev":476.5,"rev_growth":4.7,"s":"SPECIALITY","source":"product","why":"shares brand/indian/multi"},{"ebitda_margin":33.0,"fy":"FY26","growth_basis":"7y CAGR","mcap":88801,"name":"THE INDIAN HOTELS CO. LTD","pat":2247.2,"pat_growth":34.2,"pe":39.5,"rev":9689.2,"rev_growth":11.5,"s":"INDHOTEL","source":"industry","why":"same industry (Leisure Services)"},{"ebitda_margin":31.9,"fy":"FY26","growth_basis":"3y CAGR","mcap":44224,"name":"INDIAN RAIL TOUR CORP LTD","pat":1393.4,"pat_growth":11.5,"pe":31.7,"rev":5214.9,"rev_growth":13.8,"s":"IRCTC","source":"industry","why":"same industry (Leisure Services)"},{"ebitda_margin":35.8,"fy":"FY26","growth_basis":"2y CAGR","mcap":34853,"name":"ITC HOTELS LIMITED","pat":821.3,"pat_growth":39.5,"pe":42.4,"rev":4139.4,"rev_growth":36.4,"s":"ITCHOTELS","source":"industry","why":"same industry (Leisure Services)"}],"target":{"ebitda_margin":15.1,"fy":"FY26","growth_basis":"YoY","mcap":14404,"name":"DEVYANI INTERNATIONAL LTD","pat":-42.5,"pat_growth":-564.9,"pe":null,"rev":5611.5,"rev_growth":37.7,"s":"DEVYANI"}},"plants":"","pre":0,"products":"{\"YUM Brands\": [\"KFC\", \"Pizza Hut\"], \"Franchisee Brands\": [\"Costa Coffee\", \"New York Fries\", \"Sanook Kitchen\"], \"Own Brands\": [\"Vaango\", \"The Food Street\", \"Biryani By Kilo\", \"Goila Butter Chicken\"]}","promises":[],"promises_total":0,"promoters":[{"name":"Mr. Ravi Jaipuria","role":"Promoter; Non-executive Chairman of the Board"},{"name":"Mr. Varun Jaipuria","role":"Promoter; Non-executive, Non-Independent Director; son of Mr. Ravi Jaipuria"},{"name":"RJ Corp Limited","role":"Holding company and part of the Promoter/Promoter Group; held 57.98% of paid-up equity share capital as of March 31, 2026 (Promoter/Promoter Group together held 61.36%)"}],"related":"[{\"name\": \"RJ Corp Limited\", \"relation\": \"related party\", \"note\": \"Holding company of Devyani International Limited; part of the Promoter/Promoter Group\"}, {\"name\": \"Devyani International (Nepal) Private Limited\", \"relation\": \"subsidiary\", \"note\": \"Wholly-owned subsidiary; Nepal operations\"}, …","risks":[{"note":"Exposure to economic slowdowns, inflationary pressures and shifts in consumer sentiment that may adversely affect discretionary spending and overall demand. Mitigated through a multi-tier pricing architecture, continuous menu innovation and value engineering to offset input-cost inflation, and ongoing monitoring of macro indicators to recalibrate pricing and promotions. Management explicitly flags that near-term uncertainties relating to consumer demand and input costs will require close monitoring.","risk":"Macroeconomic & market risk - discretionary demand sensitivity"},{"note":"Revenue volatility from festive cycles, regional demand patterns and evolving consumer preferences can hit store-level performance and capacity utilisation. Addressed with advanced analytics and predictive demand modelling, agile workforce and inventory planning, and targeted campaigns and product bundling to drive traffic in lean periods.","risk":"Demand variability & seasonality"},{"note":"Process inefficiencies, execution gaps or dependency on key personnel may disrupt operations or impact service quality across a 2,000-plus store network. Mitigated by clearly defined standardised operating procedures across locations, a strengthened governance structure with defined roles, accountability and escalation mechanisms, and leadership pipeline development with structured succession planning.","risk":"Operational & organizational risk - execution and key-person dependency"},{"note":"Supplier concentration, logistics constraints, geopolitical factors and commodity price volatility can affect availability and cost structures for a food business. Managed through a diversified, geographically distributed supplier network, long-term strategic sourcing agreements for cost predictability, technology-enabled supply-chain visibility with real-time inventory tracking, and periodic supplier audits and risk assessments.","risk":"Supply chain & procurement risk"},{"note":"Attracting, retaining and upskilling talent in a high-attrition industry can impact service delivery and growth plans; the BRSR shows permanent-employee turnover remaining elevated. Mitigated by structured recruitment, onboarding and performance management, continuous investment in training and leadership development, engagement and wellness programmes with performance-linked incentives, and a focus on diversity, equity and inclusion.","risk":"Human capital risk in a high-attrition industry"},{"note":"As the business becomes increasingly technology-enabled - with digital ordering, kiosks, analytics and data-driven HR and supply-chain systems - risks relate to system downtime and scalability limitations, making resilience of digital platforms central to operations.","risk":"Technology & information security risk"},{"note":"Identified in the BRSR double-materiality assessment as a material risk with negative financial implications: any compromise in food safety and quality may result in customer dissatisfaction along with regulatory scrutiny, notices and penalties. Mitigated through rigorous hygiene standards from preparation to storage under strict SOPs, regular evaluation of stores by franchisors, third-party agencies and regulators, and sourcing of all critical raw materials from GFSI-compliant suppliers.","risk":"Restaurant food safety and quality"},{"note":"BRSR-identified material risk with negative financial implications: inadequate waste management can result in regulatory non-compliance, and improper disposal may lead to environmental contamination of soil, water and air. Governed by the Company's Environment and Waste Management Policy covering waste reduction, segregation, timely disposal, hygiene standards and regulatory compliance.","risk":"Waste management and environmental compliance"}],"rpt":{},"rpt_date":"","s":"DEVYANI","sector":"Consumer Services","sk":{"as_of":"2026-06-17","cagrs":[{"cagr":"~8%","period":"Fiscal 2020-Fiscal 2025","segment":"India cruise market"},{"cagr":"~20-25%","period":"Fiscal 2026-Fiscal 2031","segment":"India cruise market (projected)"}],"drivers":"Rising disposable incomes and increasing trend of experiential travel|Growing domestic cruise adoption and awareness of cruising|Improving port infrastructure and last-mile connectivity|Increase in total number of itineraries and destinations|Very low current penetration (0.01%) providing headroom","industry":"Leisure Services","market_size":[{"metric":"India overnight ocean & coastal cruise market","value":"Rs 8,301 million","year":"Fiscal 2025"},{"metric":"India overnight ocean & coastal cruise market","value":"Rs 5,764 million","year":"Fiscal 2020"},{"metric":"India overnight ocean & coastal cruise market (estimated decline ~12% YoY)","value":"Rs 7,325 million","year":"Fiscal 2026"},{"metric":"India overnight ocean & coastal cruise market (projected)","value":"Rs 18,200-22,500 million","year":"Fiscal 2031"},{"metric":"India cruise market penetration","value":"~0.01% (vs ~5.7% North America)","year":"2025 (Fiscal 2026)"}],"overview":"The overnight ocean and coastal cruise industry in India is a nascent, high-growth segment of cruise tourism. Per the issuer-commissioned CRISIL Report (May 2026), the domestic market was valued at approximately Rs 8,301 million in Fiscal 2025 (up from Rs 5,764 million in Fiscal 2020, ~8% CAGR) but is estimated to have declined ~12% to Rs 7,325 million in Fiscal 2026 amid macro/fuel/geopolitical headwinds, before a projected sharp expansion. India's cruise penetration (~0.01%) is far below developed markets (~5.7% in North America), indicating large untapped potential. The global cruise …","peer_note":"drhp_issuer_selected — issue-driven, not exhaustive","peers":[],"schemes":"Cruise Bharat Mission (launched September 2024) - aims to double cruise passenger traffic by 2029; modernise/develop cruise terminals, marinas and circuits|Amrit Kaal Vision 2047 (11 themes incl. cruise sector promotion, green initiatives, technological innovation)|Maritime India Vision 2030","structure":"Nascent and under-penetrated domestic cruise market; globally the industry is mature and highly consolidated with the top four operators (Carnival, MSC, Norwegian Cruise Line, Royal Caribbean) holding 70-80% share. Indian-operated cruise lines face structural disadvantages vs foreign operators in …"},"strategies":"Transform DIL into a diversified, technology-enabled and consumer-centric multi-brand food services platform - the 'House of Brands' strategy - spanning multiple cuisines, formats, occasions and price points|Complete and integrate the proposed share-swap merger with Sapphire Foods India Limited to consolidate operations, unify marketing strategy and strengthen the supply chain across KFC and Pizza Hut, paving the way for 'DIL 2.0'|Pursue a two-track expansion approach: calibrated, cluster-based store expansion at KFC with leaner store formats, and measured re-calibration of Pizza Hut openings …","strengths":"Largest franchisee of Yum! Brands in India and Nepal, with exclusive franchise rights across key markets reinforcing competitive advantage|Disciplined expansion built on clear capital allocation principles focused on demand density, unit economics and long-term returns|Integrated multi-brand operating model creating operating leverage through shared sourcing, supply chain, distribution and overheads|Portfolio of globally loved brands plus differentiated homegrown concepts enabling participation across cuisines, occasions and consumer segments|Scale advantages in site selection, supply chain …"},"meta":{"co":"DEVYANI INTERNATIONAL LTD","isin":"INE872J01023","s":"DEVYANI","sector":"Consumer Services"},"momentum":{"stock":{"mem":0,"s":"DEVYANI","iid":"16296","c":"","g":4,"sig":"","d":169,"t":"","a":null,"lm":"","r":null,"ft":"","m":17725,"cl":143.76,"ad":"2026-08-14","prev_stage":1,"prev_zone_vpe":null,"fam":"","pdd":"","atd":"","ins":{"has":true,"thesis":"Devyani delivered a strong Q4FY26 with KFC posting its best SSSG in 14 quarters driven by a strategic pivot to dine-in, while guiding for 200-225 net new stores in FY27. The proposed merger with Sapphire Foods remains on track for completion by FY26 end, promising scale synergies, though near-term margins face pressure from elevated coffee/cocoa costs and gas volatility.","co":"DEVYANI INTERNATIONAL LTD","q":"Q4FY26","cd":"2026-05-21","sec":"Quick Service Restaurants (QSR